      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The taxon historically called \"Salmo caspius\" (Caspian salmon) is now regarded as a subspecies of brown trout (Salmo trutta caspius) that is anadromous and spawns in tributary rivers, not in the sea itself.",
      "evidence": "Recent ichthyological revisions and IUCN assessments reclassify Salmo caspius as a brown‑trout subspecies; its reproductive behavior is documented as riverine spawning.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "No extant true salmon species (Salmo salar or Oncorhynchus spp.) have established, self‑sustaining populations in the Caspian Sea, and introduced salmon have not persisted.",
      "evidence": "Comprehensive fisheries surveys of the Caspian basin (FAO, regional research institutes) list only sturgeon, carp, and trout; salmon are absent.",
      "weight": 0.15,
